    "evidence_text": "A mutation in Orai1 causes immune deficiency by abrogating CRAC channel function\n\nBoth approaches converged on a novel protein that we call Orai1, which contains four putative transmembrane segments.\n\nWe propose that Orai1 is an essential component or regulator of the CRAC channel complex.\n\nNef is physically recruited into the immunological synapse and potentiates T cell activation early after TCR engagement.\n\nNef potentiates T cell activation by an unknown mechanism, probably by optimizing the intracellular environment for HIV replication.\n\nThis Nef-induced priming of TCR signaling pathways occurred independently of calcium signaling and involved a very proximal step before protein kinase C activation.\n\nApproximately 5-10% of the total cellular pool of Nef is localized within lipid rafts.\n\nThis recruitment was dependent on the N-terminal domain of Nef encompassing its myristoylation.\n\nNef did not increase the number of cell surface lipid rafts or immunological synapses.\n\nThe activation of T cells is the fundamental on switch for the adaptive immune system.\n\nCa2+ signaling is essential for T cell activation and starts as initial, short-lived, localized Ca2+ signals.\n\nThe second messenger nicotinic acid adenine dinucleotide phosphate (NAADP) forms rapidly upon T cell activation and stimulates early Ca2+ signaling.\n\nJunctophilin-4, a component of the endoplasmic reticulum-plasma membrane junctions, regulates Ca2+ dynamics in T cells.\n\nHowever, the molecular composition of these junctions in T cells remains poorly understood.\n\nAccordingly, expression of this cytoplasmic fragment of JP4 inhibited SOCE.\n\nHere we investigate the signaling pathway(s) that lead to PTH induction of E4bp4 mRNA expression.\n\nTen and 100 nm PTH induced maximum E4bp4 expression in MOBs.\n\nMoreover, PTH (3-34) that does not activate cAMP signaling did not increase E4bp4 expression.\n\nIn contrast, 80 microg/kg PTH (3-34) had no effect.\n\nInhibition of receptor-mediated calcium influx in T cells by unsaturated non-esterified fatty acids.\n\nChem.\n\nFlow-cytometry analysis showed that UFAs do not interfere with antibody-receptor binding.\n\nThe relevance of these findings and possible mechanisms for inhibition by UFAs of receptor-mediated Ca2+ influx were discussed.\n\nControl of NFAT Isoform Activation and NFAT-Dependent Gene Expression through Two Coincident and Spatially Segregated Intracellular Ca2+ Signals\n\nHow closely related coexpressed transcription factors are differentially activated remains unclear.\n\nNFAT1 is stimulated by sub-plasmalemmal Ca2+ microdomains, whereas NFAT4 additionally requires Ca2+ mobilization from the inner nuclear envelope by nuclear InsP3 receptors.\n\nNFAT1 is rephosphorylated (deactivated) more slowly than NFAT4 in both cytoplasm and nucleus, enabling a more prolonged activation phase.\n\nInstead, effective sustained physiological activation of NFAT4 is tightly linked to oscillations in nuclear Ca2+.\n\nOur results show how gene expression can be controlled by coincident yet geographically distinct Ca2+ signals, generated by a freely diffusible InsP3 message.\n\nGenome-wide RNAi screen of Ca(2+) influx identifies genes that regulate Ca(2+) release-activated Ca(2+) channel activity.\n\nThe nonreceptor tyrosine kinase Syk coupled CRAC channel opening to protein kinase C and ERK activation.",
